import Native

{-
   The twelve `Real` bodies that are one instruction at AVX-512 - Analysis-Avx512.md §3.7, and
   Design-Replacements.md for what a `@replaces` is.

   `Math.yana` writes each of these in bit arithmetic at the type's own width, and that is what every
   build below this level runs. What this file does is replace the *vector* ones with the instruction
   the machine has had since Skylake-SP, declared in `Native/Intrinsic/X86.yana`. The scalar
   instances are untouched: `vgetexpss` and its siblings exist, but the scalar bodies they would
   replace are two or three instructions already and the win is not worth a second pair of
   declarations.

   The file's name is the whole of the gating, on `Memory.native.avx512.yana`'s terms: a body behind
   a compile-time `if hasAvx512()` would still be resolved, would still reach lowering in a build
   with the IR optimizer off, and the backend would then refuse instructions the target cannot
   encode. A file selected on the level is simply not read.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   What each replacement costs today, and what it costs here

   | body | `Math.yana` | here |
   | --- | --- | --- |
   | `exponentOf` | bitcast, shift, `and`, convert, subtract - 5, and the `F64` pair needs the whole `vecDoubleFromWholeBits` round trip, which is 6 more | `vgetexpps`/`pd` - 1 |
   | `significandOf` | bitcast, `and`, `or`, bitcast - 2, plus two pooled constants | `vgetmantps`/`pd` at control 0 - 1 |
   | `scaleByExponent` | two clamps, two power-of-two builds, two multiplies - 15 or more | `vscalefps`/`pd` - 1 |
   | `fract` | `floor` and a subtraction - 2 | `vreduceps`/`pd` at control 1 - 1 |
   | `minNumber`/`maxNumber` | two comparisons, two selects and an `or` - 5 | `vrangeps`/`pd` at control 4 or 5 - 1 |

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Where the answers differ, which is only outside what `Real` defines

   `exponentOf` and `significandOf` are documented for a **positive normal argument** and for nothing
   else, and on that domain the instruction and the bit arithmetic agree bit for bit. Outside it they
   do not, and the instruction is the better answer every time: `vgetexp` gives a subnormal its true
   exponent where the shift gives -126 or -1023, a zero minus infinity where the shift gives the same
   bias, an infinity infinity and a NaN a NaN. Nothing in this library reads either answer, since
   every caller guards its own zero and its own negative (`Math.yana`'s `log` and `pow` do it in the
   line above the call), so this is a difference an author can rely on at `avx512` and not below it -
   which is why `Real` keeps documenting the narrow domain.

   `scaleByExponent` is required to **saturate**, and both spellings do: the two clamped
   multiplications reach an infinity or a zero by ordinary arithmetic, and `vscalef` saturates to an
   infinity above the range and underflows gradually below it. What they do not share is the answer
   for a NaN exponent - the clamped pair multiplies by two powers of two built from a truncated NaN,
   and `vscalef` answers a NaN - and again nothing reads it: `exp` and `exp2` guard the argument
   before the call.

   `fract` is `x - floor(x)` in both, and `vreduce` at control 1 is that computation exactly - M is
   zero, so the rounding is applied to the whole value, and the mode is round-down.

   `minNumber` and `maxNumber` promise that a NaN operand is ignored and say nothing about which zero
   comes back when both operands are zero. `vrange` at control 4 and 5 keeps the first promise and
   makes its own answer to the second, and the sign it gives the answer is the chosen operand's -
   which is what the control byte's high half has to say, and the one thing here that was wrong the
   first time it was written. See the note above those two.
-}

@replaces
fn exponentOfVecFloat(value: Vec(F32)) -> Vec(F32) = X86.getExponent(value)

@replaces
fn exponentOfVecDouble(value: Vec(F64)) -> Vec(F64) = X86.getExponent(value)

-- Control 0: the significand normalized into [1, 2) with the source's own sign - which is what
-- `Real` documents `significandOf` as, for the positive normal arguments it is defined on.
@replaces
fn significandOfVecFloat(value: Vec(F32)) -> Vec(F32) = X86.getMantissa(value, 0)

@replaces
fn significandOfVecDouble(value: Vec(F64)) -> Vec(F64) = X86.getMantissa(value, 0)

@replaces
fn scaleByExponentVecFloat(value: Vec(F32), exponent: Vec(F32)) -> Vec(F32) =
    X86.scale(value, exponent)

@replaces
fn scaleByExponentVecDouble(value: Vec(F64), exponent: Vec(F64)) -> Vec(F64) =
    X86.scale(value, exponent)

-- Control 1: no fraction bits kept before the rounding, and the rounding is down - so the answer is
-- `x - floor(x)`, in [0, 1) for every finite argument.
@replaces
fn fractVecFloat(value: Vec(F32)) -> Vec(F32) = X86.reduce(value, 1)

@replaces
fn fractVecDouble(value: Vec(F64)) -> Vec(F64) = X86.reduce(value, 1)

{-
   Control 4 and 5: the minimum and the maximum, each taking its answer's sign from **the operand the
   comparison chose** rather than from the first one.

   The sign control is the half of this byte that is easy to get wrong, and the wrong answer is not a
   sign - it is the wrong number. `minNumber(nan, 3.0)` selects `3.0`, and with the sign taken from
   the first operand it comes back as `-3.0`, because x86 builds its default quiet NaN with the sign
   bit *set*: `0.0 / 0.0` is `0xffc00000`. That is what the first version of this file did and what
   `Math.Real`'s `fractionAndOrder` case caught at this level and not below it.

   Bits 1:0 are still the comparison - 0 the minimum, 1 the maximum - so the two bytes are 0b0100 and
   0b0101.
-}
@replaces
fn minNumberVecFloat(lhs: Vec(F32), rhs: Vec(F32)) -> Vec(F32) = X86.range(lhs, rhs, 4)

@replaces
fn minNumberVecDouble(lhs: Vec(F64), rhs: Vec(F64)) -> Vec(F64) = X86.range(lhs, rhs, 4)

@replaces
fn maxNumberVecFloat(lhs: Vec(F32), rhs: Vec(F32)) -> Vec(F32) = X86.range(lhs, rhs, 5)

@replaces
fn maxNumberVecDouble(lhs: Vec(F64), rhs: Vec(F64)) -> Vec(F64) = X86.range(lhs, rhs, 5)
